
radio's android body is declarative: the group is a for over the choices, the
container is the one `direction` asks for, and each option is a RadioButton
beside its label in a Row aligned on its center. Nothing about radio is keyed
on its name in compose_ir.go any more, so what this asserts is the override's
own shape.

Choices written one by one stay a loop, like a list of them: Kotlin is a language
target, so the loop is emitted and its own compiler decides whether to unroll
it. Three copies of a RadioButton is what this used to be. direction is the
other way round -- "horizontal" is a Row and the default vertical a Column, and
both fold at build time so no runtime branch survives.

Compose passes onClick nothing, so the override names the option itself: the
@change handler's `e` is the label of the option that was clicked, read off the loop
variable.

deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `RadioButton\(selected = \(pick == "small"\)` -- a literal options list is left as a loop, not unrolled into one RadioButton per option
deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `val e = ` -- the handler reads the loop variable directly; there is nothing Compose handed it to bind
deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `"horizontal" == "horizontal"` -- direction is compared at build time, so neither branch's test reaches the output

win32 inlines each radio, and its override's buttons read `#button` from
their own click. Each spliced copy of a library override takes a handle of
its own for a node it reads, as it does for one it calls a method through --
three radios sharing one handle were refused as ambiguous.

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    go {
        win32
    }
}

component main node {
    var pick = "medium"
    var chosen = ""
    var more = ["a", "b"]

    vbox {
        radio(style={padding=4px}, direction=horizontal, value=pick, @change(e) { chosen = e }) {
            choice(label="small", value="small")
            choice(label="medium", value="medium")
            choice(label="large", value="large")
        }
        radio(style={padding=5px}, value=pick) {
            choice(label="one", value="one")
            choice(label="two", value="two")
        }
        radio(value=pick) {
            for var m = more {
                choice(label=m, value=m)
            }
        }
    }
}

window {
    main
}
